==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[creme de la creme]]

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|En-us-crème de la crème.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-uk-creme de la creme.ogg|Audio (UK)|lang=en}}

===Etymology===
{{borrowing|fr|crème de la crème|lit=cream of the cream|lang=en}}.

Its origins have been traced to the decade 1840–1850,<ref>Dictionary.com, http://dictionary.reference.com/browse/creme+de+la+creme</ref> but its popularity in modern English (it is less common in modern French)<ref>StackExchange, English Language & Usage, http://english.stackexchange.com/questions/41325/origin-of-cream-of-the-crop</ref> is attributed to its use by the lead character in Muriel Spark's 1961 novel ''[[wikipedia:The Prime of Miss Jean Brodie (novel)|The Prime of Miss Jean Brodie]]'', in which the intensely authoritative girl's school teacher refers to her charges with this epithet.<ref name="general editor: Peter Boxall, author of entry: Drew Milne p.538">{{cite book
 | last = Boxall
 | first = Peter (gen. ed., Drew Milne entry author)
 | title = 1001 Books You Must Read Before You Die
 | year = 2006
 | publisher = [[Quintet Publishing]]
 | isbn =978-1-84403-417-8
 | page=538
 }}</ref> The line from the novel reads, "'I am putting old heads on your young shoulders,' Miss Brodie had told them at that time, 'and all my pupils are the crème de la crème.'"<ref>Wikiquote: Muriel Spark, http://en.wikiquote.org/wiki/Muriel_Spark</ref>

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{context|idiomatic|singulare tantum|lang=en}} Best of the best; something [[superlative]]. The [[very]] [[best]].
#: ''To be an astronaut you must be the '''crème de la crème'''.''

